<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">C. Rajagopalachari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rajaji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">C. Rajagopalachari, known as Rajaji, was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> leader, lawyer and writer who served as Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1948 to 1950, the only Indian to hold the office. A close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, he had been premier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> Presidency from 1937 to 1939 and was later Chief Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a>. In 1959 he founded the Swatantra Party in opposition to Nehru's economic policies.</p></div>
